what would be great?

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.740
Why don't we go ahead
and give everyone

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:09.450
an exact idea of how we
go through acquisitions?

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.670
And we'll go through.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.350
I can't drive as well
backwards as I would like to.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.530
But we go through
and tell exactly,

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.150
hey, any company that we
would like to work with us,

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.437
here's exactly
how we would work.

00:10:21.437 --> 00:10:23.020
Here's how your first
days would work.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:24.610
Here's how we value you.

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.130
Here's how we'd bring you in.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.780
And so that transparency
is something

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:32.620
that, again, works across here
and the rest of the company.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:35.280
So let me jump back in
and click to the next one,

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.070
because the values are useful.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.750
This is what we
had to deal with.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.793
We start as a company more
or less in this create space.

00:10:42.793 --> 00:10:44.460
So when we had to run
our SaaS offering,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.110
we're only needing
to deal with Git.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.170
It's interesting.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:48.390
It's really powerful.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:49.740
It's our core.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:52.080
But now as we've expanded
and we deliver this level

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.410
of complexity, we have to be
able to-- at a regular basis--

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.600
be able to run that for
every single customer.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.280
So that means we have
to run pipelines.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:02.100
It's going to mean that we
have to have a great managing.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.500
We have to manage and run
all of this extremely well.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.515
So companies expect to be
able, if they live in a silo,

00:11:08.515 --> 00:11:10.390
and when they come to
GitLab, they're asking,

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:12.290
hey, how do I break
down those barriers?

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:15.270
How can I run and
develop concurrently

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:17.238
instead of sequentially?

00:11:17.238 --> 00:11:18.280
That was an issue for us.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:19.197
It was a prize for us.

00:11:19.197 --> 00:11:21.097
So as we went through
the process here,

00:11:21.097 --> 00:11:23.180
we used to, of course,
have a bunch of other tools

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.840
too, because we only
started in the Git space.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.790
When we moved on to
planning, CI, monitoring,

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.290
we were surprised
actually ourselves

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:34.165
when we put these things
all together exactly

00:11:34.165 --> 00:11:37.030
what the compression
of that cycle time was,

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:38.350
which is wonderful.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.990
But when we run that product now
as a SaaS for every customer,

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:43.477
that thing better run
perfectly every time.

00:11:43.477 --> 00:11:44.560
Because they are betting--

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:47.590
our customers are betting
their business on GitLab.com

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:51.230
as the ability to ship
product every single day.

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:52.900
So that's the part
that we had to make

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.323
sure we executed perfectly.

00:11:55.323 --> 00:11:55.990
One other thing.

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.130
We talked a bit
about transparency,

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.330
which obviously matters.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:02.327
But I'm going to-- you're going
to see a theme here throughout.

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.094
That's fine.

00:12:08.094 --> 00:12:11.580
I'm going to move to the
next slide here real quickly.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.980
Give you a little context of
what we had to migrate over.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.330
So when we moved over,
we had to move everything

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:19.162
from the source code.

00:12:19.162 --> 00:12:20.870
But we had to move
all the Kanban boards.

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:22.513
We had to move our
project management.

00:12:22.513 --> 00:12:24.680
Likewise, we wanted to roll
out-- when we made this,

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:26.630
we want to make it easy to do
things like, if you look right

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:28.150
in the middle, review apps.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:29.990
Want to make it easy
for our customers

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:31.910
to be able to deploy
new apps exactly how

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.440
it's running instantaneously.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:37.708
And so that was the
last part we did there.

00:12:37.708 --> 00:12:39.500
That said, I'm going
to quickly get it over

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.260
to Andrew, because you guys
want to know why we moved over.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:43.910
We're going to spend
most of our time--

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:48.180
some people will know GitLab
as the left-hand side here.

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.850
As an end user, that's
mostly what the UI UX is.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.020
Today, the rest of it, we're
going to spend on this part.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:55.770
How do we migrate all
the different pieces

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:59.670
of the complex application
that is GitLab?

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.910
Couple of really important
things that we focused on.

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.370
First off, we wanted to
be suitable, obviously,

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:06.370
for mission critical.

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.698
That's really important for
us to grow our business.

00:13:08.698 --> 00:13:09.615
We needed performance.

00:13:09.615 --> 00:13:11.550
We needed consistency in price.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:14.250
That was the reason we
moved from Microsoft.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.610
We saw better performance--
we'll highlight that--

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:20.500
better consistency, and
significantly better price.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:21.990
The other part, if you're
thinking about the other part,

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:22.500
was culture.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:25.160
So more and more development
is landing in cloud data.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.910
If you're here, you've
heard this all day.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:27.800
You heard Anthos.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.150
I don't have to
explain any of that.

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.850
However, for us, when we want
to work with a partner that's

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:35.802
going to help us
understand and work deeply

00:13:35.802 --> 00:13:38.010
around those technologies,
there's not a better place

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.060
for us to go than was Google.

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:42.900
And so that was part
of why we moved.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:46.490
Last one was-- and that hit
in part two and part three.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:49.350
So part three, a lot of
those are technologies,

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:51.000
that while we use
them very deeply,

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.860
the advantage for us
as a company worked

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:55.380
with Google early was we
have real deep insight

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:56.730
into how that works.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:00.150
Likewise, Google uniquely
was willing to walk us

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.100
through exactly how
Google developers develop.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:05.880
So we get kind of a future
look into perhaps how

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.435
other large
companies, or probably

00:14:08.435 --> 00:14:10.560
one of the fastest shipping
companies in the world,

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:13.240
actually does their
DevOp life cycle.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.280
That said, the last one
I'm going to turn over

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:18.250
to Andrew here in a second.

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:21.730
And last one, I think, speaks
pretty much for itself.

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:22.780
We're a small company.

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:25.390
In doing so, we were going
to write all up about it.

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:27.098
Andrew's going to tell
you how we did it.

00:14:27.098 --> 00:14:28.806
And I think that's
pretty helpful for us.

00:14:28.806 --> 00:14:30.598
So with that, I'm going
to let Andrew chat.

00:14:30.598 --> 00:14:32.520
This is probably who
you wanted to talk to.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:33.820
And much appreciated.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:34.862
ANDREW NEWDIGATE: Thanks.

00:14:34.862 --> 00:14:35.740
Thanks, Brandon.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.143
My name is-- [LAUGHS].

00:14:38.143 --> 00:14:39.310
My name is Andrew Newdigate.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:41.080
I'm an engineer at GitLab.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:44.290
I helped lead the team
that moved GitLab.com

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:47.560
from Azure to GCP.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:49.810
Now, Brandon told you
a little bit about why

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.310
we wanted to move to GCP.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.825
And I'm going to tell you a
little bit about how we did it.

00:14:53.825 --> 00:14:54.700
And I need a clicker.

00:14:57.470 --> 00:14:57.970
Cool.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:01.640
So the goal of the project
was really, really simple.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.180
It's basically move
GitLab.com to GCP.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:06.430
So we could state
it really simply.

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:10.590
The problem was how could
we go about doing this?

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:13.240
Now, Brandon discussed our
company values earlier.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:14.920
I'm going to try
to illustrate how

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:18.430
we use these company values
to apply them to the way

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:19.150
that we work.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:22.570
And in particular, I'm going
to focus on efficiency, or how

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:26.140
we love boring
solutions, iteration,

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:29.980
or how we focus on shipping
the minimum viable change,

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:33.950
and finally on transparency.

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.160
So the first
iteration of the plan

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:39.730
was really a combination
of a really boring solution

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:41.620
and a minimum viable plan.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:45.220
So we considered whether we
could just stop the whole site,

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:48.480
copy all the data
from Azure to GCP,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.070
and then switch the DNS
over to point at GCP

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.800
and then start
everything up again.

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:57.760
So the problem is that we
had too much data to do this

00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:59.200
within a reasonable time frame.

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:02.050
So we had, at the time,
about half a petabyte

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:04.870
in total of data on Azure.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:06.460
And once we'd shut
down the site,

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:09.340
we'd need to copy all of
this data between two cloud

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.230
providers over the internet.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:13.510
And then once the
copy was complete,

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:15.400
we'd need to verify
the data and make sure

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.410
that it was all 100% correct.

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:21.040
And only then could we
start the site up again.

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.310
This plan meant that GitLab.com
could be down for several days

00:16:24.310 --> 00:16:25.290
on end.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:27.880
And considering that thousands
and thousands of people

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:32.440
rely on GitLab on a daily
basis, this didn't really work.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:34.690
So we went back to
the drawing board

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:38.380
and started considering
what our options would be.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:40.450
So around about
the same time, we

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:43.193
were working on another
feature at GitLab,

00:16:43.193 --> 00:16:44.110
and that's called Geo.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:48.180
And what Geo allowed us
to do is develop a feature

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:50.290
for our enterprise
customers so that they

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:54.490
could replicate a GitLab
instance from one site

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:55.680
to another.

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.580
And this is really
useful because you

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:01.000
can use it for faster
access at the second site.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:02.320
It's also redundancy.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.450
And you can use it for
site disaster recovery.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:08.619
So Geo works by doing an
initial synchronization

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:12.910
of all of your data from the
first site to the second site.

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:15.069
And then afterwards,
it makes sure

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:16.930
that everything
is synchronized so

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:19.069
that any change
on the first site

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:21.670
is replicated to the
second in near real time.

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:25.670
So you always have a
duplicate copy of your data.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:29.780
Now, GitLab.com is
not a fork of GitLab.

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:31.780
GitLab.com is GitLab.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:34.840
And so Brandon mentioned that
on the 22nd of every month,

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:36.490
we do a release of GitLab.

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:39.620
And the first place that
that release is deployed to

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.200
is GitLab.com on the
7th of the month.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:46.210
So two weeks before we
release the software,

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.700
we deploy it on our own
servers on GitLab.com.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:50.590
And we even use the
same package installer,

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:54.670
called GitLab Omnibus, to
install onto GitLab.com.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:57.070
And this means that
any feature that we

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:59.500
build for our self-managed
customers is also

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:02.560
available for us to
use on GitLab.com.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:05.350
And so we hoped that by
utilizing these replication

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:07.405
capabilities that we
were building for Geo,

00:18:07.405 --> 00:18:11.110
we could migrate the
entire GitLab.com site

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.285
to a secondary instance in GCP.

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:21.100
So a new plan was formed
using Geo for replication.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:24.180
And the secondary environment
would be GCP, as I mentioned.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:27.260
And then what we would do
is synchronize all the data.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:29.590
And this could
possibly take weeks.

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:30.632
Or it could take months.

00:18:30.632 --> 00:18:32.340
It didn't really
matter, because the site

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:36.110
would be available throughout
the synchronization process.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:41.950
And then once all the data
had been synchronized to GCP,

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:46.150
we could verify it and make sure
that it was all 100% correct.

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:49.480
And then we could just
promote the GCP environment

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.610
and make it our new primary.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:55.390
This had many advantages
over the initial plan.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:57.250
So obviously,
firstly, GitLab.com

00:18:57.250 --> 00:19:01.090
would be up for the
synchronization.

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:03.310
And then we would have
a period of downtime,

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:06.018
but it would be very short,
maybe an hour or two.

00:19:06.018 --> 00:19:08.060
And the other thing that
was great about it is we

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:08.830
could time box it.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.140
So we could know
exactly how long

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:14.870
that downtime was going to be.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:16.610
Then, since the new
environment would

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:19.660
be running all of our production
data, we could verify it.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:20.860
We could do QA.

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:24.430
We could build automated
QA tools, manual QA tools.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:26.020
We could do load testing on it.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.585
And obviously, we could
verify all of the data

00:19:28.585 --> 00:19:30.340
before the failover.

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:34.060
And then finally, because
we had this migration,

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:36.250
we could use the migration
as an opportunity

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:41.350
to do ultimate QA of GitLab Geo.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:44.883
And so basically, if it could
work for us on GitLab.com,

00:19:44.883 --> 00:19:47.050
it would pretty much work
for any other customer who

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:48.330
wanted to use Geo.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:50.547
And we could be
confident in that.

00:19:50.547 --> 00:19:51.880
I'm just going to quickly grab--

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:53.922
BRANDON JUNG: And that
goes back to the iteration

00:19:53.922 --> 00:19:55.980
and the testing.

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:58.675
And that goes back to our value
of iteration, testing, dog

00:19:58.675 --> 00:19:59.740
fooding.

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.685
We make sure before
our customers touch it.

00:20:01.685 --> 00:20:03.310
We're going to make
sure it runs great.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.230
And then it gets into
the largest deployment

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:08.177
in the world, which means if
you're running it yourself,

00:20:08.177 --> 00:20:09.760
you can be fully
confident that that's

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.500
something that's ready to go.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:12.610
ANDREW NEWDIGATE: Cool.

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:14.402
So around about this
time, we were actually

00:20:14.402 --> 00:20:16.800
working on another
major project,

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:19.330
and that was called
Cloud Native GitLab.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:21.430
And this project is
basically about building

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.470
a set of Helm
charts for GitLab so

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:26.200
that we could deploy
GitLab inside a Kubernetes

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:27.690
environment.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.610
And much like Omnibus is our
official package installer

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:35.530
for installing GitLab outside
a Kubernetes environment,

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:37.240
the Helm charts in
Cloud Native GitLab

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:40.150
is our official
installer for installing

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:43.860
GitLab inside a
Kubernetes environment.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:46.550
So instead of provisioning
the new environment

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:50.240
using Chef and Omnibus,
we thought maybe

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:53.090
what we could do is
deploy it into Kubernetes

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:56.960
using Google Kubernetes
engine and our Helm charts.

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:59.870
And so we evolved the plan
with another iteration

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:05.240
so that we used Cloud Native for
setting up the new environment.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:08.323
The problem was
that this approach--

00:21:08.323 --> 00:21:09.740
it became apparent
that there were

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:11.940
problems with this
approach as we went along.

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:15.170
So the changes that we needed
to make to GitLab.com--

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:16.730
at least to GitLab the product--

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:18.540
were extensive.

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:21.620
And we realized that there was
going to be some major rework.

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:24.320
And so it was difficult to align
the time frames of the Cloud

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:27.080
Native GitLab project
and the GCP migration

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:29.705
in order to get them both
delivered within the time

00:21:29.705 --> 00:21:32.190
frames that we wanted.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:36.470
So we went back, and we sort
of went to the next iteration,

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:41.660
where we went back to using
Omnibus for provisioning

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:42.853
the new environment.

00:21:42.853 --> 00:21:44.270
And we also realized
that it would

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:47.300
be worthwhile to migrate
as much data as possible

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:50.630
to object storage with
Google Cloud Storage.

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:55.680
And so obviously, a lot of the
data on GitLab is Git data.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:58.250
But there's also huge
amounts of non-Git data--

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:03.470
build artifacts, CI
repositories, CI images,

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:04.380
that sort of thing.

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:08.510
And what we could do is take it
off NFS, where it was stored,

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.160
and move it into object storage.

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:13.820
And doing this would allow us
to reduce the risk and the scale

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:15.980
of the Geo migration.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:19.170
And additionally, moving
that data into object storage

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:22.010
and eliminating
NFS from our stack

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:25.033
meant that in future, when
we moved to Kubernetes,

00:22:25.033 --> 00:22:26.450
it would be much
easier because we

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:29.720
didn't have to worry about NFS.

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:32.150
So the steps for the
migration were now

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:33.870
fairly straightforward.

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:36.410
We would provision
a new environment

00:22:36.410 --> 00:22:38.870
with GitLab Omnibus,
and we would set it up

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:41.360
as a Geo secondary in GCP.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:43.730
And then we would replicate
all the data from GitLab.com

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.360
in Azure to GCP.

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.820
We'd wait for the initial
synchronization to complete.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:52.130
And then when it
had completed, we'd

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:55.790
test the new environment with
automated manual testing tools.

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:59.700
And we'd obviously verify that
all the data is 100% correct.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:01.200
And then finally,
once that's done,

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:04.100
we'd failover to
the GCP environment,

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:06.230
promote it to primary,
and we'd be done.

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:14.640
So we began to set up
the new environment.

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:17.400
And we started the
replication process

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:19.620
and just started
replicating non-Git content

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:21.250
to object storage.

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:24.450
Now, being remote means that
we can't spend our time playing

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:27.060
sword fights in the hallways
while we wait for things.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:30.330
So we used the time
to be productive,

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:33.222
and we started
implementing other features

00:23:33.222 --> 00:23:34.180
in our new environment.

00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:36.900
So we set up Identity
Aware Proxies

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:38.670
for all of our
internal web apps.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:42.190
We set up Cloud Key Management
Service for security.

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:45.660
And we also did a major revamp
of our logging infrastructure,

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.570
moving to structured
logging wherever we could

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:53.520
and switching to fluentd and
Google Pub/Sub for logging.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.980
And for short-term logging,
we use Stackdriver and ELK.

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:59.040
And for long-term logging,
we stored our data

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:01.840
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:06.460
So there was only one major
unknown left in this plan,

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:09.590
and that was the actual
failover operation itself.

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.740
So unfortunately,
Geo, at the time,

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:14.170
didn't support a
failover operation.

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:18.200
So nobody knew exactly how
we would go about doing that.

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:20.500
So we needed to define
a step-by-step plan

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:22.690
to carry out this failover.

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:24.820
Now, considering the
stakes were so high,

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.700
it was essential that we got
this right the first time

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:28.870
that we tried it.

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:30.250
And how did we ensure this?

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:32.930
Well, we just kept on iterating.

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:36.610
So we set up a failover
procedure as an issue template

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:38.230
in GitLab.

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:42.490
And then each step in the
failover was a checklist item.

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:44.960
And then we started
practicing the failover.

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:47.300
And every time we
did a practice,

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:49.300
we'd create a new issue
from the template.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:50.590
We'd run through it.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:52.360
And anything that
went wrong would

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.810
get fed back into the
template as a merge request.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:57.760
So the whole team
would have a say,

00:24:57.760 --> 00:24:59.800
and we could discuss it
on the merge request.

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:03.140
And through this very
tight feedback loop,

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:09.730
we could rapidly improve
the failover procedure.

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:17.120
So this animation over here
shows the main failover

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:18.470
documents.

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:22.250
And there were over 140
changes in that documents.

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:23.690
So every time we
tried something,

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:25.160
we would submit a change.

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:30.020
And over time, the document got
longer and longer and longer.

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:33.650
And when we started,
every time we tried it,

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:36.230
it would basically not work.

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:39.980
But every time we did it, we
would improve it a little.

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.470
And we kept doing this until
we were totally confident

00:25:42.470 --> 00:25:44.237
that the failover would work.

00:25:44.237 --> 00:25:46.070
And then when we were
confident, we actually

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:47.810
introduced another
level of difficulty

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:50.570
by starting to mess
with the failover,

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:54.110
having somebody secretly
shutting down servers

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:57.292
or rebooting Redis or shutting
down [INAUDIBLE] primary.

00:25:57.292 --> 00:25:59.000
And then the team
would have to recognize

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:03.380
that that had happened
and take it into account--

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:06.080
detect it and improve
it and then carry on

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:07.370
with the failover.

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:09.230
And once we were
doing that, we knew

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:11.540
that we were totally
confident and that it was

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:14.930
time to carry out the failover.

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:18.530
So we let Google know, and
they assembled an amazing team

00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:21.570
to help us with the failover.

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:22.070
Sorry.

00:26:22.070 --> 00:26:23.870
I'm just going to
have some water.

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:26.690
BRANDON JUNG: One fun note
on that previous slide,

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:29.630
if you're ever interested
in exactly how that works,

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:30.430
it's an open issue.

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:32.180
So anyone that's
interested, you can watch

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:34.020
exactly how we cut it over.

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:37.320
You can go look on exactly how
we went through that process.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:41.210
And so you're going to detail
by detail, ad nauseum, what

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:42.648
that kind of work takes.

00:26:42.648 --> 00:26:44.690
So if it's something you
all are trying to solve,

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:46.815
again, we put all our
documents out there

00:26:46.815 --> 00:26:48.440
because we want to
collaborate and help

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:50.660
everyone else along the
same journey we've been,

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.722
and obviously get your feedback.

00:26:52.722 --> 00:26:53.930
Not necessary on the cutover.

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:55.350
That's long been since done.

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:56.600
But in the rest of what we do.

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:58.650
So it matters an
enormous amount to us.

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:01.980
Hopefully that becomes something
also that's a usable document.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:04.400
And I think pretty unusual
that a company is live

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.232
stream how they
make the migration,

00:27:06.232 --> 00:27:08.690
as well as giving everyone else
visibility on how you might

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:10.120
be able to do it yourself.

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:11.760
ANDREW NEWDIGATE: Cool.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:12.260
Oh, sorry.

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:19.070
So I skipped a
little bit farther.

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:21.370
So Google assembled an
amazing team of people

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:23.850
to support us on the day.

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:26.650
And we knew they'd be available
if we needed any assistance.

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:28.840
Luckily, the failover
went really smoothly,

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:31.750
and we didn't experience
any major problems.

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:37.750
On the Monday following the
failover, as traffic started

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:41.260
rising on the site, we started
seeing some performance issues.

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:43.360
And working with
Google, we realized

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.750
that our Gitaly servers,
which run all the Git

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:50.730
operations for GitLab, were
struggling a little bit.

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:53.860
And we spoke to Google.

00:27:53.860 --> 00:28:00.040
They suggested we move
from N1-highmem-8 machines

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:03.130
to N1-standard-32 machines.

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:05.530
And so we rotated the
fleet out to those.

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:10.380
And after that, it was
plain sailing the whole way.

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:13.240
So going back to the
goals of the project,

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:19.270
did we make GitLab.com suitable
for mission critical workloads?

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:24.660
So firstly, let's consider
availability on GitLab.com.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:27.480
So as an external
tool, we use Pingdom

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:29.900
to monitor the site
externally and tell us

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:31.930
if it sees any failures.

00:28:31.930 --> 00:28:34.830
And so I pulled the data
from Pingdom from before,

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:38.730
when we were in Azure, and then
afterwards, when we're in GCP.

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:40.650
And this is a
graph of the number

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:45.130
of errors we saw per day
in those two environments.

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:47.130
So that is a few months before.

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:50.608
And that is everything
after the failover.

00:28:50.608 --> 00:28:51.650
And the blue background--

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:52.320
I'm colorblind.

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:54.270
I can't see it being
blue in this image,

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:56.400
but hopefully it is.

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:57.780
The blue background
is the period

00:28:57.780 --> 00:28:59.790
when we were running in GCP.

00:28:59.790 --> 00:29:05.730
So prior to our failover, we had
about 8.2 Pingdom errors a day.

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:08.660
And after the failover, we
had just one error a day.

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:15.130
So Pingdom also
reports availability.

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.860
And this, you can see,
is our availability

00:29:17.860 --> 00:29:22.810
in the year leading up to
the migration is 99.61%,

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.698
and afterwards was 99.93.

00:29:25.698 --> 00:29:27.490
Now, some people might
think that those two

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:30.520
numbers are really
similar, but they're not.

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:34.540
99.61 is 39 minutes
of downtime a week.

00:29:34.540 --> 00:29:37.040
And afterwards, we've seen
7 minutes of downtime.

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:39.250
So we still have some
improvements to make.

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:40.720
But it's a lot
better than it was.

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:47.175
So the other way that
we can compare the sites

00:29:47.175 --> 00:29:49.320
is with the performance
of GitLab.com

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:52.050
before and after
moving to Azure.

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:54.150
So one way that we
could do this is

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:58.080
with latency histogram, which
shows us the performance

00:29:58.080 --> 00:29:59.910
of requests to GitLab.

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:01.770
And what I did for
this was I took data

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:04.440
for one week before the
migration and one week

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:06.780
after the migration so
that there weren't too

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:08.520
many other changes
to the application.

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:12.240
We were pretty much
comparing like with like.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:17.880
And what you can see is that the
GCP line drops off much quicker

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:21.790
and has a much, much
lower tail of values.

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:24.600
And so what this shows us
is that our GitLab instance

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:28.470
in Azure was slower, and
our GitLab instance in GCP

00:30:28.470 --> 00:30:29.830
was much faster.

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:34.560
And we also had far fewer
unpredictable values,

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:37.600
so random values that took 30
seconds or random requests that

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:40.690
took 30 seconds.

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:43.410
And then finally, we
have, as Brandon said,

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:44.730
we have all the documents.

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:46.590
We have the issue tracker.

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:48.900
We have the project
documentation

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:53.520
all available over here for
everyone to take a look at.

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:56.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

